  { z: 1.9, tag: 'meas', title: 'Cosmic noon',
    text: 'Averaged over the universe, new stars were being born about nine times faster than today. Star formation has been winding down ever since.' },
  { z: 5.3, tag: 'meas', title: 'Starlight strips the hydrogen',
    text: 'Ultraviolet light from the first galaxies had been knocking the electrons off the hydrogen atoms that filled space. About 1.1 billion years after the Big Bang the last neutral patches were gone. Space had been see-through to ordinary light all along; now ultraviolet light could cross it too.' },
  { back: 1.3e10, tag: 'meas', title: 'The Milky Way\'s oldest stars',
    text: 'Our galaxy began to take shape. The oldest stars we can date in the Milky Way formed around now, give or take half a billion years, less than a billion years after the Big Bang.' },
  { z: 7.6, tag: 'meas', title: 'The first giant black holes',
    text: 'Black holes of a billion Suns are already shining as quasars, only about 700 million years after the Big Bang, and smaller ones are seen even earlier. How they grew so fast is not yet understood.' },
  { id: 'momz14', z: 14.44, tag: 'meas', title: 'The farthest galaxy yet seen',
    text: 'The most distant galaxy confirmed so far (the record as of October 2026), MoM-z14, shines from about 280 million years after the Big Bang. Its light has been stretched more than fifteen times on its way to the Webb telescope.' },
  { tc: 1.5e8, tag: 'model', title: 'Cosmic dawn: the first stars',
    text: 'Somewhere in the dark, between about 100 and 250 million years after the Big Bang, clouds of hydrogen and helium collapsed and lit up as the very first stars. They were probably huge, hot and short-lived, and they forged the first heavier elements such as carbon and oxygen. None has been seen directly yet.' },
  { fn: 'darkages', tag: 'calc', title: 'The cosmic dark ages',
    text: 'After the first glow faded, there were no stars at all. Ten million years in, the afterglow of the Big Bang had cooled to about {K} kelvin, roughly the temperature of boiling water, and it kept on cooling. For over a hundred million years the universe held only dark hydrogen and helium gas, slowly being pulled into clumps by the gravity of dark matter: an invisible material that outweighs ordinary matter five to one and whose nature is still unknown.' },
  { id: 'cmb', z: 1089.92, tag: 'meas', title: 'The first light breaks free',
    text: 'The universe cooled to about 3,000 kelvin, cool enough for electrons and nuclei to join into atoms. The glowing fog cleared over about 100,000 years and light could travel freely; we still see it today as the cosmic microwave background.' },
  { fn: 'equality', tag: 'calc', title: 'Matter overtakes light',
    text: 'Until now most of the universe\'s energy was in radiation: light plus neutrinos. As space stretched, radiation thinned out faster than matter, and from this point matter\'s gravity could start gathering the seeds of future galaxies.' },
  { tcSec: 180, tag: 'model', title: 'The first elements are forged',
    text: 'For a few minutes the whole universe was a nuclear furnace at a billion degrees. Protons and neutrons fused, leaving about three quarters hydrogen and one quarter helium by mass. The helium measured in ancient gas matches this prediction; the lithium does not, and nobody knows why.' },
  { tcSec: 1, tag: 'model', title: 'Neutrinos break free; the last antimatter vanishes',
    text: 'At about one second and ten billion degrees, ghostly particles called neutrinos stopped interacting and have streamed freely ever since. Over the next few minutes, electrons and their antimatter twins, positrons, destroyed each other, leaving a small surplus of electrons.' },
  { tcSec: 1e-4, tag: 'model', title: 'Matter wins, narrowly',
    text: 'As the universe cooled, protons and antiprotons destroyed each other in pairs. For every billion pairs, about one particle of matter was left over, and everything we see is made of those leftovers. Why there was any imbalance at all is one of the great unsolved questions.' },
  { tcSec: 2e-5, tag: 'model', title: 'Quarks lock into protons',
    text: 'Before this moment the universe was a soup of free quarks and gluons at two trillion degrees. As it cooled, the quarks were locked together in twos and threes; the threes that last are protons and neutrons. Most of the mass of everything you can touch comes from this binding energy.' },
  { tcSec: 1e-11, tag: 'model', title: 'Particles gain their mass',
    text: 'The Higgs field, which fills all of space, settled into the value it has today. Electromagnetism and the weak nuclear force, which had acted as one, went their separate ways, and particles such as electrons and quarks gained mass. This is about as far back as laboratory experiments have tested the physics.' },
  { tcSec: 1e-32, tag: 'spec', title: 'Inflation: a sudden stretch',
    text: 'The leading idea is that space ballooned unimaginably fast for a tiny fraction of a second, then filled with hot particles. It would explain why the universe looks so smooth and where the seeds of galaxies came from, but it is not proven, and its place on this clock is a convention: nobody knows when it happened.' },
  { id: 'begin', tcSec: 1e-36, tag: 'spec', title: 'Where known physics stops',
    text: 'Run the clock back further and our theories of gravity and quantum physics stop working together, by 10⁻⁴³ seconds at the latest. Nobody knows what happened at time zero, or whether there was a time zero at all.' }
